def longest_palindrome(s: str) -> str:
    def expand_around_center(left: int, right: int) -> str:
        while left >= 0 and right < len(s) and s[left] == s[right]:
            left -= 1
            right += 1
        return s[left + 1:right]

    if not s:
        return ""

    start, end = 0, 0
    for i in range(len(s)):
        len1 = len(expand_around_center(i, i))
        len2 = len(expand_around_center(i, i + 1))
        max_len = max(len1, len2)
        if max_len > end - start:
            start = i - (max_len - 1) // 2
            end = i + max_len // 2

    return s[start:end + 1]
```

This solution uses the "expand around center" technique to find the longest palindrome centered at each character and between each pair of characters. It keeps track of the indices of the longest palindrome found and returns the substring from those indices. The time complexity is O(n^2), where n is the length of the string, as each expansion can take up to n steps. The space complexity is O(1) since it only uses a few extra variables.
